      "text": "To find the area of Mateen's backyard, we need to use the information given about the perimeter and the number of times he needs to walk it to cover a kilometer (1000 meters). Let's denote the length of the backyard by \\( L \\) and the width by \\( W \\).\n\nFirst, let's determine the perimeter of the backyard. The problem states that walking the length 25 times will cover 1000 meters. This means:\n\\[ 25L = 1000. \\]\nSolving for \\( L \\), we get:\n\\[ L = \\frac{1000}{25} = 40. \\]\nSo, the length \\( L \\) of the backyard is 40 meters.\n\nNext, let's find the width of the backyard using the information that walking the perimeter 10 times will also cover 1000 meters. The perimeter \\( P \\) of the rectangle is given by:\n\\[ P = 2L + 2W. \\]\nWalking the perimeter 10 times means:\n\\[ 10P = 1000. \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 10(2L + 2W) = 1000. \\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[ 2L + 2W = 100. \\]\nSince we already know \\( L = 40 \\), we substitute this value into the equation:\n\\[ 2(40) + 2W = 100. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ 80 + 2W = 100. \\]\nSolving for \\( W \\), we get:\n\\[ 2W = 20, \\]\n\\[ W = 10. \\]\nSo, the width \\( W \\) of the backyard is 10 meters.\n\nNow, we can find the area \\( A \\) of the backyard using the formula for the area of a rectangle:\n\\[ A = L \\times W. \\]\nSubstituting the values of \\( L \\) and \\( W \\), we get:\n\\[ A = 40 \\times 10 = 400. \\]\nTherefore, the area of Mateen's backyard is \\(\\boxed{400}\\) square meters.",
